Public domain
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The decimal-mode arithmetic in Arithmetic6502.java (ADC/SBC NMOS BCD flag
behavior) is traced from an algorithm authored by Bruce Clark, explicitly
released to the public domain by its author:

    "Written by Bruce Clark. This code is public domain."
    http://www.6502.org/tutorials/decimal_mode.html

No source code or binary from Clark's reference is included in this
project -- the algorithm it describes was independently reimplemented in
original Java.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Documentation and reference sources (courtesy attribution)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following were consulted as documentation/reference material during
development. No source code or binaries from these sources are included
in this project; the underlying facts and algorithms they document were
independently reimplemented in original Java.

  - John West and Marko Mäkelä, "64doc" -- NMOS decimal-mode and
    illegal-opcode reference. https://www.nesdev.org/6502_cpu.txt
  - Graham ("oxyron.de") -- community-standard illegal-opcode reference
    table. https://www.oxyron.de/html/opcodes02.html
  - groepaz/hitmen, "NMOS 6510 Unintended Opcodes -- No More Secrets"
  - The Apple II Documentation Project. https://www.apple2.org.za/
  - applelogic.org -- Apple II hardware reverse-engineering and preserved
    ROM/PROM images. http://www.applelogic.org/
  - Jim Sather, "Understanding the Apple II" -- hardware reference for
    6502 bus timing, the Disk II controller's logic state sequencer, and
    related soft-switch behavior. No text from the book is reproduced;
    its documented timings, state tables, and quirks were independently
    reimplemented in original Java.
  - Virtual ][ (Gerard Putter) -- a commercial, closed-source emulator
    used purely as a black-box behavioral reference: specific timing and
    soft-switch quirks were confirmed by breakpoint-based tracing of its
    own running behavior, then independently reimplemented here. No code,
    binaries, or disassembly from Virtual ][ are included or were
    examined. https://www.virtualii.com/
  - fileformats.archiveteam.org -- the DSK/DO/PO raw sector-image format
    entry, used to confirm that format's lack of any header or metadata
    capacity. https://fileformats.archiveteam.org/
  - Steve Chamberlin ("bigmessowires.com"), "The Amazing Disk II
    Controller Card" -- general-audience explanation of the Disk II
    controller's hardware design, consulted while researching how
    writing to disk works at the hardware level.
    https://www.bigmessowires.com/2021/11/12/the-amazing-disk-ii-controller-card/
  - mikedaley/web-a2e wiki, "Disk System Internals" -- another project's
    own documentation of its Disk II controller model, consulted
    alongside the sources above for the same research.
    https://github.com/mikedaley/web-a2e/wiki/Disk-System-Internals
  - MAME's own documentation of its soft-reset (F3) versus hard-reset
    (Shift+F3) distinction, consulted when designing this project's own
    Reset/Reboot toolbar controls. No MAME code is included; the
    documented behavioral distinction was independently reimplemented.
    https://docs.mamedev.org/
  - Steve Chamberlin ("bigmessowires.com"), "Apple II Copy Protection" --
    a second, separate article from the same author already credited
    above, consulted specifically to confirm the "no more than two
    consecutive zero bits" physical constraint on real Apple II disk
    encoding, used to detect a never-formatted disk track from its own
    stored content.
    https://www.bigmessowires.com/2015/08/27/apple-ii-copy-protection/
  - CiderPress2 project, "Nibble Image Files" documentation -- consulted
    alongside the above for the same zero-bit-run constraint and its
    history across Apple II disk controller revisions.
    https://ciderpress2.com/formatdoc/Nibble-notes.html
  - Eric Smith, posting to the comp.sys.apple2 mailing list (April 1999)
    -- a primary, firsthand technical account of the P6/P6A PROM
    revision and the zero-bit-run constraint it changed, consulted
    alongside the above.
    http://ana-3.lcs.mit.edu/~jnc/cctalk/1999-April/0686.html

